    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The calibration looks fine on paper, but the data spikes are really weird. Like, it's not just a steady drift, it's these sudden, erratic jumps that don't follow a clear pattern. It makes it feel like something is interfering with the electronics or the reading momentarily. Any idea why the data might act so glitchy during those specific pressure shifts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sudden spikes that appear erratic and don't follow a clear pattern suggest transient interference or disturbances, potentially from the pressure changes themselves. At such extreme depths, the sensor housings would be under intense pressure; these conditions, combined with rapid pressure shifts, might lead to temporary misalignment of the sensor elements or electrical interference. Vibration and shock generated during the rapid descents, known as transient shock waves, could momentarily disrupt sensor readings as well. Evaluating the dynamic performance of the electronics under simulated extreme pressure conditions would provide insights. In addition, performing real-time sea-trial tests with your AUV, where you can monitor the behavior of the sensors and equipment during controlled pressure variations, would help in understanding and potentially mitigating these glitches. Isolating the electronics from vibratory and shock sources, or implementing a protective housing that dampens such effects, might offer additional stability to your measurements.",
